Several reviews have summarized the research efforts in gas adsorption applications for MOFs, such as hydrogen and methane storage, and carbon dioxide capture.37–42 Recently, Sholl and co-authors43 contributed a review on experimental applications of MOFs in large-scale carbon dioxide separations in which both adsorption-based and kinetic separations of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) are included. Adsorption-based separations rely on the fact that gases adsorbed in nanopores have much higher densities than those of the gases in bulk phases. Kinetic separations use differences in adsorption affinities and differences in diffusivities of gases in a porous adsorbent. Kinetic separations are often used in membrane-based applications.44,45 In this review, we will focus on the progress and challenges in using MOFs for adsorption-based \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) capture including both experimental and simulation studies. First, research on high pressure \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) storage in MOFs will be reviewed. Then, the \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) adsorption at subatmospheric pressure and selective \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) adsorption in MOFs will be presented and analyzed. Next, the key strategies aiming to increase \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) capacities and/or selectivities in MOFs will be discussed and summarized into several categories. At last, current challenges toward using MOFs in \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) capture, such as cost and stability, will be discussed as well.
## 2 \( \mathbf{CO}_{2} \) adsorption in MOFs at high pressures
\( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) storage by adsorbents is an economical and relatively mature method considering the low cost of equipment and the possible recycling uses of the captured \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \).46–48 Millward and Yaghi published a pioneering work in which \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) isotherms up to 42 bar were reported for nine MOF materials as shown in Fig. 2.49
Several MOFs have higher saturated \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) capacities than benchmark adsorbents such as zeolites NaX (or 13X) and activated carbon MAXSORB. Particularly, MOF-177, which is composed of \( \mathrm{Zn} \) and a large linker \( 4,4',4'' \)-benzene-1,3,5-triyl-tribenzoic acid \( \left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTB}\right) \), has an unprecedented \( 33.5\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1} \) \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) capacity at \( 25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) and 35 bar. The authors ascribed this large \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) capacity to the large pore space enclosed in MOF-177. Their results also showed that the saturated \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) capacities of the MOFs are qualitatively correlated with their surface areas.
MIL-53 (Al) and MIL-53 (Cr) are interesting materials because of their ''breathing'' crystal structures induced by adsorption of \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) molecules as shown in Fig. 3.50,51 The structure to the left shows the hydrated form in which the pores are slightly deformed due to hydrogen-bond interactions between the hydrogen atoms of the water molecules and the oxygen atoms of the carboxylates and the \( \upmu_{2} \)-hydroxyl groups. After water is removed through heating, the pores will return back to the structure on the right with more open porosity. Bourrelly et al.44–51 found that \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) molecules will initially be adsorbed to the hydroxyl groups in MIL-53 (Al) and this will cause shrinkage of the structure. A further increase in the \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) pressure will lead to reopening of the pore structure. They reported that MIL-53 (Al) has a \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) capacity of \( 10.4\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1} \)
at 30 bar and \(304\;\mathrm{K}\) , which is well above those of conventional zeolites and comparable with microporous carbons. Substituting Al with \(\mathrm{Cr}\) in MIL-53 essentially did not cause any change in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacity. Thallapally et al.52 synthesized an interpenetrated MOF which also shows breathing motion upon solvent loss and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) inclusion. This breathing MOF has a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacity of \(7.1\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at 30 bar and \(298\;\mathrm{K}\) , which is similar to that of the NaX zeolites under the same conditions.
Llewellyn et al.53 reported that MIL-101 \((\mathrm{Cr})\) , another MOF from Materials of Institute Lavoisier (MIL), shows a high \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacity, which is about \(18\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at 50 bar and \(304\;\mathrm{K}\) . Furthermore, the authors activated MIL-101 \((\mathrm{Cr})\) with ethanol and \(\mathrm{NH_{4}F}\) to increase its surface area and pore volume which leads to a record \(40\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\,\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacity at 50 bar and \(304\;\mathrm{K}\) .
Matzger and coauthors54 synthesized UMCM-1 using zinc nitrate and two different ligands, 1,3,5-tris(4-carboxyphenyl)- benzene \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTB}\right)\) and terephthalic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\right)\) . UMCM-1 has a high surface area \((4100\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and giant pore volume \((2.141\;\mathrm{cc}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) . Mu et al.55 measured \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) isotherms at three different temperatures for UMCM-1 and they obtained a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacity of \(23.5\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at 24 bar and \(298\;\mathrm{K}\) .
Farha et al.56 used computational modeling to design a metal–organic framework, NU-100, with a particularly high surface area. Then they successfully obtained a matched MOF through the experimental synthesis, with a high BET surface area \((6143\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) . The NU-100 is composed of copper centers and a large hexatopic carboxylate ligand \(\left(\mathrm{LH}_{6}\right)\) which is shown in Fig. 4. Furthermore, the NU-100 has a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacity of \(46.4\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at 40 bar and \(298\;\mathrm{K}\) .
Recently, Yaghi and coauthors reported several MOFs with ultra-high porosity.57 Particularly, MOF-210 exhibits the highest BET and Langmuir surface area (6240 and \(10\,400\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and pore volume \((3.60\;\mathrm{cc}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and \(0.89\;\mathrm{cc}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) of MOF materials reported so far. Basically, they achieved the ultra-high porosity by expanding the sizes of organic linkers from terephthalic acid (BDC) in IRMOF-1 to \(^{4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}}\) -(benzene-1,3,5-triyl-tris (benzene-4,1-diyl)) tribenzoate (BBC) in MOF-200. The BBC linker is even larger than \(^{4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}}\) -benzene-1,3,5-tritytribenzoate (BTB) which is used to synthesize MOF-177 as shown in Fig. 4. More importantly, they achieved a new record \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacity of \(54.5\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at 50 bar and \(298\;\mathrm{K}\) for MOF-200 and MOF-210. For monodisperse cubic nanoparticles to have an external surface that is equal to these two MOFs, the size of the nanoparticle would have to be only 3 to \(6\;\mathrm{nm}\) , which may
not be large enough for practical use. This indicates that the surface areas of MOF-200 and MOF-210 are close to the ultimate limit for solid materials and so is the \(54.5~\mathrm{mol~kg}^{-1}\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacity at 50 bar and \(298~\mathrm{K}\).
Results reported so far about \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption in MOFs at high pressures are summarized in Table 1. It is clear that \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacities at high pressures depend on surface areas and pore volumes of the MOFs. Increasing surface areas and pore volumes of MOFs is an effective way to enhance their \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) storage capabilities. Therefore, many large organic linkers with multiple benzene rings and extended length, such as the BTB and BBC linkers mentioned above, have been used to synthesize MOFs with extra-large surface areas and pore volumes. In addition to tailoring chemical compositions and pore structures such as using larger organic linkers, activating MOFs with supercritical fluid is another option to increase surface areas and pore volumes. Supercritical activation can eliminate the solvent surface tension at temperatures and pressures above the critical point which will prevent the pore collapse that would otherwise occur upon removal of organic solvents by heat. Nelson et al.64 reported that MOFs have much larger surface areas and pore volumes after solvent exchange than after thermal evacuation. Supercritical drying (ScD) can help thoroughly remove residual solvent inside a MOF and increase their surface area to an even larger value than using solvent exchange.
## 3 Sub-atmospheric pressure and selective \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) adsorption in MOFs
## 3.1 \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) adsorption at sub-atmospheric pressures
There are at least two applications where separation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from other gases using MOFs is of interest. They are separation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from sour natural gas wells and separation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from power-plant flue gas.41 The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) partial pressure is much lower than atmospheric pressure for the second application. Therefore, it is important to study \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption in MOFs at sub-atmospheric pressures.
Yazaydin et al.65 used both experiments and simulation to screen MOFs for the highest \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacities at about 0.1 atm. They found that \(\mathrm{Mg/DOBDC}\) and Ni/DOBDC (also known as Mg-MOF-74 and Ni-MOF-74 or CPO-27-Mg and CPO-27-Ni) have the highest \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacities at 0.1 atm and \(298~\mathrm{K}\), which are \(5.95~\mathrm{mol~kg}^{-1}\) and \(4.07~\mathrm{mol~kg}^{-1}\), among the 14 MOFs that they considered. The results are shown in Fig. 5.
A correlation between the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacity and the heat of adsorption at sub-atmospheric pressures for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption in MOFs was also reported. In contrast to \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption in MOFs at high pressures, there is no correlation between the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacity and the surface area or the free volume. Therefore, they concluded that MOFs with a high density of open metal sites, such as \(\mathrm{Mg/DOBDC}\) and Ni/DOBDC, are promising in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture from flue gas in which \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) partial pressure is about 0.1 atm. Liu et al.36 found that Ni/DOBDC has a higher \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacity than \(\mathrm{NaX}\) and 5A zeolites at 0.1 atm, and \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). In addition, water does not affect \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption in Ni/DOBDC as much as in \(\mathrm{NaX}\) and 5A zeolites, and it is much easier to remove water from Ni/DOBDC by regeneration. In other words,
\(\mathrm{Ni/DOBDC}\) can adsorb more \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) than traditional zeolites under the same moist conditions. Aprea et al.66 reported the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) isotherms at room temperature of CuBTC. Their results showed that CuBTC has a higher \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacity at atmospheric pressure and a lower isosteric heat of adsorption than zeolites \(\mathrm{NaX}\), which means CuBTC could be more suitable for fixed-bed \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption applications than zeolites \(\mathrm{NaX}\). Caskey et al.67 found that metal substitution in the DOBDC series can impact their \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacities at the low-pressure region significantly. This metal substitution effect may be caused by the differences in the ionic character of the metal-oxide bonds in the DOBDC-series MOFs.65
## 3.2 \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) adsorption over \(\mathbf{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathbf{CH_{4}}\)
Large capacities at sub-atmospheric pressures are essential for application of MOFs in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption. However, selectivity is a more important factor since \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is always mixed with other gases in practical applications.39 The selectivity for gas A relative to gas B is defined by \(S_{\mathrm{AB}}=(x_{\mathrm{A}}/x_{\mathrm{B}})(y_{\mathrm{B}}/y_{\mathrm{A}})\), where \(x_{\mathrm{A}}\) and \(x_{\mathrm{B}}\) are the mole fractions of gases A and B in the adsorbed phase, and \(y_{\mathrm{A}}\) and \(y_{\mathrm{B}}\) are the mole fractions of gases A and B in the bulk phase, respectively. Selective adsorption of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) has attracted extended attention because of the urgent need to separate \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from flue gas. Similarly, selective adsorption of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) in MOFs is also of interest considering the potential application in natural gas upgrade. Although we will try to address \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) adsorption in MOFs in sequence, it is not necessary nor our purpose to treat them as two distinct topics because many research studies have investigated both the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) separations together.
Li et al.68 synthesized a robust zeolitic MOF material that selectively adsorbs \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\). This selectivity may be due to the small channels in the zeolitic MOF, which distinguish the two gases with kinetic diameters (\(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), \(3.3\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\); \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\), \(3.64\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\)) similar to the molecular sieve effect observed in zeolites 4A. Seven MOFs, including CuBTC, MIL-47 (V), IRMOF-1, IRMOF-12, IRMOF-14, IRMOF-11, and IRMOF-13, were studied for the separation performance of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) by Liu and Smit using Grand Canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulations.69 In all the
MOFs they considered, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is more preferentially adsorbed than \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) with CuBTC showing the highest selectivity. IRMOF-1, IRMOF-12, and IRMOF-14 with large cubic pores give the lowest \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption selectivities up to 20 bar. They found that pore size plays an important role in the selective adsorption of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and the reason is that both \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) molecules have quadruple moments so the electrostatic interactions will help increase the adsorption of both components. The effect of the chemistry of the materials, i.e. effects of the electrostatic interaction, becomes less evident compared to the effects of pore size on selective adsorption of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\). Wu et al.70 constructed a Li-modified IRMOF-1, chem-4Li MOF, which was obtained by substituting all the hydrogen atoms by O–Li groups in the aromatic rings of IRMOF-1 as shown in Fig. 6. The chem-4Li MOF was found to have a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity of 395 \((\mathrm{CO}_{2}:\mathrm{N}_{2}=15.6:84.4)\), which is two orders of magnitude larger than that of the original IRMOF-1. The main reason is due to the stronger electrostatic interactions between the framework atoms and the gas molecules induced by the introduction of lithium. Yang et al.71 reported a similar enhanced \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) selectivity phenomenon for Li modified IRMOF-16.
MIL-53 (Al) is known as a MOF that has flexible structures, which can affect \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption. As mentioned before, besides hydration, adsorption of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) leads to contraction of the MIL-53 (Al) framework and formation of a narrow pore. Finsy et al.72 found that up to 5 bar, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) strongly interacts with the framework hydroxyl groups in MIL-53 (Al), while \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) is adsorbed in an unselective way. Further adsorption of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules at higher pressures reopens the framework and the adsorption mechanism become unselective. As a result, the average separation factor between \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) decreases from 7 to 4. In order to improve the selectivity of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) in MIL-53(Al), Couck et al.73 synthesized amino-MIL-53 (Al) using 2-aminoterephthalic acid as the linker and the presence of the amino groups together with the hydroxyl groups drastically enhances the affinity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), resulting in an almost infinite selectivity of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) according to their experimental data.
HKUST-1 is another MOF that has been extensively studied for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption. Yang and Zhong74 found that the HKUST-1 has ordered microdomains with different electrostatic field strengths in its structure, which can greatly enhance the separation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) because the two components have largely different electrostatic interactions with the HKUST-1. Martin-Calvo et al.75 simulated the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) adsorption process in HKUST-1 and they reported that the siting of
the molecules in HKUST-1 provided the high adsorption selectivity towards \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). However, Hamon et al.76 did not observe a significant influence of the adsorption sites on the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) selectivity at low pressure in their experimental results. Hamon and coauthors also measured a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) delta loading of \(7.37\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) between the production step at \(1.0\;\mathrm{MPa}\) and the regeneration step at \(0.1\;\mathrm{MPa}\) for the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}-\mathrm{CH}_{4}-\mathrm{CO}\) (70–15–15) separation by using HKUST-1. This selectivity is significantly higher than that of zeolite \(\mathrm{NaX}\) and activated carbon under the same conditions.
Keskin and Sholl77 predicted that IRMOF-1 has a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) selectivity of 2.5 based on their simulation results of a mixture composed of equal parts of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\). They also found that mixture effects significantly affect the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) selectivity for IRMOF-1 under conditions relevant for natural-gas applications. Therefore, examining only single component gases will not be sufficient for understanding the properties of MOFs in practical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) separation applications. Perez et al.78 found that mixing \(30\%\) IRMOF-1 with a Matrimid1 polymer can enhance the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) selectivity of IRMOF-1 to 29 at \(308\;\mathrm{~K~}\) and 2 bar. Other than mixing with a polymer, the selectivity of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) mixtures is greatly increased for IRMOF-1 by introducing lithium ions into its structure. This enhancement is due to the electrostatic potential in the materials caused by the presence of the metals.79 Recently, experimental evidence has been reported by Bae et al.80 to show that Li doping can help improve the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) selectivity to about 50 for some Zn-based mixed-ligand MOFs. They proposed two ways to incorporate Li cations into MOFs, chemical reduction or cation exchange. For the chemical reduction case, the increases in selectivity can be explained by the favorable displacement of catenated frameworks, as well as pore-volume diminution. Conversely, the selectivity enhancement is due to the desolvated-Li(charge)/\(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)(quadruple) interactions for the cation exchange method.
Another interesting MOF, \(\mathrm{Mg/DOBDC}\), has been recently studied for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) adsorption.81 The authors found that its \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacity is significantly higher than that of zeolite 13X under similar conditions but the pressure-dependent equilibrium selectivity of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) in \(\mathrm{Mg/DOBDC}\) showed a trend similar to that of zeolite 13X. The authors used the Langmuir model to fit both pure \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and pure \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) isotherms. The intrinsic selectivity of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) for \(\mathrm{Mg/DOBDC}\) at zero adsorption loading is calculated to be 283 at \(298\;\mathrm{~K~}\) based on the isotherm data and the Langmuir model parameters.
Selected results about the selectivities of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) for some MOFs are shown in Table 2. Some results of the zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs), a subclass of MOFs, were also included for reference. It is obvious that many MOFs have high \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) selectivities which are essential for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) separation from natural gas and flue gas. However, as pointed out by Sholl and coauthors, it is not intrinsically interesting that MOFs preferentially adsorb \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) or \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) because all the microporous adsorbents such as zeolites and activated carbon do unless that a MOF's adsorption selectivity and/or capacity do substantially improve upon traditional and inexpensive adsorbents.43
